,B3,B4] by the previous player. B2 is now down to one unshown tile. Discarding it could be dangerous if someone is waiting on B1/B3 to complete a sequence, but as a terminal and commonly seen tile, it may be acceptable.\n- **Play J1**: I have two J1s. No one has exposed a J1 pung yet. However, all J1 tiles are still available (4 unshown), meaning none have been discarded or used\u2014this suggests caution. But since I must discard one, J1 might be safer than honor tiles that have been actively played.\n\nReason: Among the options, **T7** stands out as the safest discard because:\n- It is part of a completed chow in my own hand, so revealing it doesn't give new strategic info.\n- T7 is not a terminal nor a wind/dragon, reducing its value for high-scoring hands.\n- Compared to B4, B2, or J3\u2014which are involved in recent melds\u2014T7 is less likely to complete a dangerous wait.\n- T3 is duplicated in my hand; breaking the pair prematurely isn\u2019t ideal unless forced.\n- J1 and J3 are dragons\u2014high-risk honors. While J1 hasn\u2019t appeared much, discarding one copy might still feed a potential dragon pung.\n- T7 balances safety and structural neutrality.\n\nAnswer: Play T7"}
